INIO|INIO’s gap up on October 27 was driven by a strong Q3 earnings report and raised 2026 guidance—not a broad-market move. Shares were reported up about 9% in premarket trading.

INNIO posted adjusted EPS of $0.15 versus the $0.12 consensus and record revenue of about $1.17 billion, up 57% year over year and above the roughly $1.05 billion estimate. Data-center equipment revenue jumped 86% to $777.6 million, reflecting strong AI-infrastructure demand.

The company also raised its 2026 revenue outlook to $3.9–$4.0 billion from $3.8–$3.9 billion and adjusted EBITDA guidance to $780–$800 million from $720–$740 million. Its equipment backlog reached a record $8.0 billion, up 142% year over year, re
CHYM|CHYM’s gap up is being driven by Chime’s Q3 earnings release on November 4 and the stronger outlook that came with it. Revenue rose 32% year over year to $718 million, above analysts’ estimates, while adjusted EBITDA reached $137 million and Chime posted its third consecutive quarter of GAAP profit.

The bigger catalyst was the “beat and raise”: Chime lifted its 2026 revenue outlook to $2.792–$2.802 billion and adjusted EBITDA outlook to $519–$524 million, and forecast Q4 revenue above consensus. Faster growth in members and traction in Chime Prime, MyPay, and lending products helped support the results. Wells Fargo also raised its price target to $42 from $39 after the report.

